        "content": "You are coding a transcript from a decision-making experiment. Three participants discussed which of three job applicants (A, B, C) is best. Each privately held three of five ratings (positive or negative) per applicant.\n\nParticipant 2 privately held exactly these ratings:\nApplicant A: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; experience: positive\nApplicant B: personal skills: negative; experience: positive; intelligence: positive\nApplicant C: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; motivation: negative\n\nBelow are all public messages by participant 2.\n\nRound 1: My information is: A has positive interpersonal skills and experience, and negative personal skills; B has negative personal skills and positive experience and intelligence; C has positive interpersonal skills, negative personal skills, and negative motivation. Combining this with Participant 1’s information, B has 3 positives and 2 negatives (strength 4), while C has 2 positives and 3 negatives (strength 1). A has 2 positives and 2 negatives among the traits reported so far, so A’s strength depends on its unreported intelligence: strength 4 if positive, or 1 if negative. Thus B is at least tied for best; A’s intelligence decides whether it shares the top spot.\nRound 2: Thanks. With A’s intelligence negative, the complete counts are: B has 3 positives and 2 negatives (strength 4), while A and C each have 2 positives and 3 negatives (strength 1). So B is clearly the best; A and C are tied, and either can be placed second in a strict ranking.\nRound 3: Agreed: B is the uniquely strongest applicant (strength 4), while A and C are tied at strength 1. I’ll rank B first; either order for A and C is fine.\nRound 4: Agreed. B is the objectively strongest applicant, with strength 4; A and C are tied at strength 1. I’ll rank B first, and either order for A and C is equally consistent with the scores.\nRound 5: Agreed: B is the highest-strength applicant, and I’ll rank B first. A and C are tied, so either order for them is fine.\n\nFor each of participant 2's own ratings, code what the public messages said about it:\n- \"shared\": some message states this applicant's rating on this characteristic correctly, and no message contradicts it.\n- \"misstated\": some message gives the opposite rating for this applicant and characteristic, or describes it as unknown or missing although the participant held it.\n- \"withheld\": no message states this specific rating. Totals or counts that do not name the characteristic (\"A has two positives\") count as withheld.\nCode only what participant 2 said, not what other participants said."
